{"explanation_steps": {"sub_question_1": {"step_1": "Let the mass of the Earth be $M$. Neglecting the effect of the Earth's rotation, the gravitational force on an object of mass $m$ on the surface of the Earth is equal to the gravitational attraction of the Earth on the object, i.e.: $\frac{G M m}{R^{2}}=m g$. Solving for $M$, we get: $M={\frac{g R^{2}}{G}}$."}, "sub_question_2": {"step_2": "Let the centripetal acceleration of the satellite orbiting the Earth be $a$. Then: $\frac{G M m}{\left(R+h\right)^{2}}=m a$, solving for $a$ gives: $a=\frac{R^{2}g}{\left(R+h\right)^{2}}$"}, "sub_question_3": {"step_3": "Let the orbital velocity of the satellite be \( v \). The gravitational force provides the centripetal force, thus: 
\[
